    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Strong Q3 and 2027 guide can\u0027t offset Texas policy risk; VST near 52-week low",
               "summary":  "Vistra\u0027s Q3 numbers were strong. Adjusted EBITDA was a record $2.38B, up from $1.58B, and on 6 Nov 2026 the company set 2027 guidance at $7.7–8.5B. The stock still fell. Gina Hinojosa won the Texas governor\u0027s race on 3 Nov after campaigning to pause new data centers, KeyBanc downgraded VST the next day, and the stock dropped 13.8%. It then slid again in the November AI sell-off and closed at $115.25 on 20 Nov, down 7.17% that day. That is about 4% above the 52-week low, and price sits below all three EMAs. The earnings outlook improved, but the political risk to Texas data-center demand is new.",
               "pros":  [
                            "Q3 adjusted EBITDA hit a record $2.38B, up more than 50% year on year, and the 2026 guidance midpoint was raised to $7.1–7.5B.",
                            "About 98% of expected 2027 output is already hedged, so the new $7.7–8.5B 2027 EBITDA range is largely locked in.",
                            "The board added $1.5B to the buyback, leaving about $2.5B to use by end-2028, with about 339M shares outstanding.",
                            "The Cogentrix deal closed in October and added about 5,500 MW of gas plants, only about 11% of it in Texas, which reduces dependence on ERCOT.",
                            "At about 13–14x forward earnings, the stock trades below its five-year average of about 15x."
